<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>elasticity</title></head><body><h1>elasticity</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Hooke's Law and Force Constant</li><li>Hooke's Law and Force Constant</li><li>Stress, Strain, Elasticity and Plasticity</li><li>Stress, Strain, Elasticity and Plasticity</li><li>Elastic Moduli — Young, Bulk, Shear</li><li>Elastic modulus: Young modulus, bulk modulus, shear modulus</li><li>Elastic Modulus (Young, Bulk, Shear)</li><li>Poisson's Ratio</li><li>Poisson's Ratio</li><li>Elastic Potential Energy</li></ul><h2>Hooke's Law and Force Constant</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Hooke's law statement:** For small deformations, the extension (or compression) of an elastic body is directly proportional to the applied force: F = kx, where F is the applied force, x is the deformation, and k is the spring (force) constant. This linear region is called the elastic limit.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Force (spring) constant k:** k = F/x, measured in N/m. It measures the stiffness of the spring — a stiffer spring has a larger k and needs a bigger force for the same extension. k depends on the material, the wire diameter, the number of coils and the length of the spring.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**What Spring constant really means:** a spring of k = 200 N/m requires 200 N to stretch it by 1 metre. To stretch by any x, you need F = 200x N. It is the slope of the F–x graph in the linear (Hookean) region.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Two ways to combine springs:** series: 1/k = 1/k₁ + 1/k₂ (softer than either); parallel: k = k₁ + k₂ (stiffer than either). Series springs stretch more for the same load — think of a long telephone-cord spring vs two short springs joined.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example:** a force of 40 N stretches a spring by 8 cm. k = F/x = 40/0.08 = 500 N/m. A 100 N force will then stretch it by x = 100/500 = 0.2 m = 20 cm.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Hooke's law on a graph:** the F–x graph is a straight line through the origin while the elastic limit is respected. Beyond it, the graph curves; after plastic deformation, a larger force is needed for the same extension or the body does not return to its original length.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Application — weight balance (spring balance):** the extension of a calibrated spring measures the applied weight. Since F = kx and weight = mg, the scale can be marked directly in newtons or kilograms.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Conditions for Hooke's law to hold:** the deformation must be small, elastic (the body returns to original shape when the load is removed), and within the proportional limit. Overstretching a spring permanently changes its k.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>F = kx holds only within the elastic limit — marked by the straight-line region of the F–x graph.</td></tr><tr><td>k measures stiffness in N/m; a larger k is a stiffer spring.</td></tr><tr><td>Series springs combine like 1/k = 1/k₁ + 1/k₂ (softer).</td></tr><tr><td>Parallel springs combine like k = k₁ + k₂ (stiffer).</td></tr><tr><td>Energy stored in a stretched spring is ½kx² — the triangle under the F–x graph.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'F = kx means force depends on extension.' ✅ Precisely that — for a spring, doubling the extension doubles the stretching force needed. The spring PULLS back with force kx (restoring force) opposite to the applied load.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'k is a property only of the material.' ✅ k depends on the geometry too (length, coil radius, number of coils, wire thickness). It is the material property (Young's modulus) together with geometry that fixes k.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A stiffer spring stretches more.' ✅ A stiffer spring stretches LESS for the same force — it has a larger k, so x = F/k is smaller.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Extension is proportional to force at all loads.' ✅ Only within the elastic (proportional) limit. Beyond it the graph bends, and past the yield point the spring deforms permanently.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Series springs are stiffer.' ✅ Series springs are SOFTER (smaller total k). Two identical springs in series have k/2; in parallel they have 2k.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Force constant and friction constant (μ) are the same.' ✅ k (N/m) relates force to displacement of a spring; μ is a dimensionless friction coefficient relating friction to normal reaction.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Hooke's law (F = kx) states that deformation is proportional to applied force within the elastic limit. The spring constant k (N/m) measures stiffness. Springs combine in series (reciprocal sum, softer) and parallel (direct sum, stiffer). The stored elastic energy is ½kx², and the law underpins spring balances, suspension systems, and simple harmonic motion.</p><hr><h2>Hooke's Law and Force Constant</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Hooke's law statement:** For small deformations, the extension (or compression) of an elastic body is directly proportional to the applied force: F = kx, where F is the applied force, x is the deformation, and k is the spring (force) constant. This linear region is governed by the interatomic potential energy well, where atoms act as if connected by springs. The proportionality limit is the point where this linear relationship breaks down, while the elastic limit is the point beyond which deformation becomes permanent (plastic).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Force (spring) constant k:** k = F/x, measured in N/m. It measures the stiffness of the spring — a stiffer spring has a larger k and needs a bigger force for the same extension. k depends on the material, the wire diameter, the number of coils and the length of the spring. For a wire: $k = EA/L$, where E is Young's modulus, A is cross-sectional area, and L is length. For a helical spring, $k = Gd^4 / (8nD^3)$, where $G$ is shear modulus, $d$ is wire diameter, $n$ is number of coils, and $D$ is coil diameter.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Restoring Force:** The spring exerts a restoring force $F = -kx$ that always opposes the displacement. This is the basis of simple harmonic motion when combined with inertia.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**What Spring constant really means:** a spring of k = 200 N/m requires 200 N to stretch it by 1 metre. To stretch by any x, you need F = 200x N. It is the slope of the F–x graph in the linear (Hookean) region.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Two ways to combine springs:** series: 1/k = 1/k₁ + 1/k₂ (softer than either); parallel: k = k₁ + k₂ (stiffer than either). Series springs stretch more for the same load — think of a long telephone-cord spring vs two short springs joined.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example:** a force of 40 N stretches a spring by 8 cm. k = F/x = 40/0.08 = 500 N/m. A 100 N force will then stretch it by x = 100/500 = 0.2 m = 20 cm.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Hooke's law on a graph:** the F–x graph is a straight line through the origin while the elastic limit is respected. Beyond it, the graph curves; after plastic deformation, a larger force is needed for the same extension or the body does not return to its original length.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Application — weight balance (spring balance):** the extension of a calibrated spring measures the applied weight. Since F = kx and weight = mg, the scale can be marked directly in newtons or kilograms.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Conditions for Hooke's law to hold:** the deformation must be small, elastic (the body returns to original shape when the load is removed), and within the proportional limit. Overstretching a spring permanently changes its k.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>F = kx holds only within the elastic limit — marked by the straight-line region of the F–x graph.</td></tr><tr><td>k measures stiffness in N/m; a larger k is a stiffer spring.</td></tr><tr><td>Series springs combine like 1/k = 1/k₁ + 1/k₂ (softer).</td></tr><tr><td>Parallel springs combine like k = k₁ + k₂ (stiffer).</td></tr><tr><td>Energy stored in a stretched spring is ½kx² — the triangle under the F–x graph.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'F = kx means force depends on extension.' ✅ Precisely that — for a spring, doubling the extension doubles the stretching force needed. The spring PULLS back with force kx (restoring force) opposite to the applied load.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'k is a property only of the material.' ✅ k depends on the geometry too (length, coil radius, number of coils, wire thickness). It is the material property (Young's modulus) together with geometry that fixes k.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A stiffer spring stretches more.' ✅ A stiffer spring stretches LESS for the same force — it has a larger k, so x = F/k is smaller.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Extension is proportional to force at all loads.' ✅ Only within the elastic (proportional) limit. Beyond it the graph bends, and past the yield point the spring deforms permanently.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Series springs are stiffer.' ✅ Series springs are SOFTER (smaller total k). Two identical springs in series have k/2; in parallel they have 2k.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Force constant and friction constant (μ) are the same.' ✅ k (N/m) relates force to displacement of a spring; μ is a dimensionless friction coefficient relating friction to normal reaction.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Hooke's law (F = kx) states that deformation is proportional to applied force within the elastic limit. The spring constant k (N/m) measures stiffness. Springs combine in series (reciprocal sum, softer) and parallel (direct sum, stiffer). The stored elastic energy is ½kx², and the law underpins spring balances, suspension systems, and simple harmonic motion.</p><hr><h2>Stress, Strain, Elasticity and Plasticity</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Stress (σ):** the internal resisting force per unit area set up in a body when external forces are applied: σ = F/A. Empirical units are N/m² or Pa (pascal). Normal stress = force perpendicular to the area (tension/compression); shear stress = force parallel to the area.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Strain (ε):** the fractional deformation produced: strain = change in dimension / original dimension. It is dimensionless. Linear strain = Δl/l, shear strain = θ (angle of shear), volumetric strain = ΔV/V.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Elasticity and plasticity:** elasticity is the property of a body to regain its original shape and size when the deforming force is removed. Plasticity is the property of remaining permanently deformed after the deforming force is removed. Bodies are elastic below the elastic limit and plastic beyond it.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Hooke's law in stress-strain form:** within the elastic limit, stress ∝ strain: σ = Eε, where E is Young's modulus (N/m²). This is the generalised Hooke's law — stress and strain are proportional until the elastic limit.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Stress-strain curve regions:** (1) linear (proportional) region up to the proportional limit; (2) elastic region up to the elastic limit (still recovers fully); (3) yielding at the yield point (permanent deformation begins); (4) plastic deformation region where the material flows; (5) ultimate tensile strength (maximum stress), then (6) fracture/breaking point.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — a wire under tension:** a steel wire of length 2 m and cross-section 1 mm² (10⁻⁶ m²) carries a 20 kg load. F = 196 N, σ = 196/10⁻⁶ = 1.96 × 10⁸ Pa. If E_steel = 2 × 10¹¹ Pa, strain ε = σ/E = 9.8 × 10⁻⁴, so extension Δl = 9.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 2 = 1.96 mm.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Types of stress & strain (NEB focus):** tensile/compressive (longitudinal), shear (tangential), and volume (hydrostatic). Each has its own modulus: Young's modulus E = σ_t/ε_l, shear modulus G = τ/θ, bulk modulus B = −P/(ΔV/V).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Ductile vs brittle materials:** ductile (copper, mild steel) show a long plastic region — they deform before breaking and are used for wires. Brittle (glass, cast iron) show little or no plastic region — they break suddenly near the elastic limit, which is why glass shatters.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Stress is force per unit area; strain is fractional deformation (dimensionless).</td></tr><tr><td>Elasticity = recovery of shape when the force is removed; plasticity = permanent deformation.</td></tr><tr><td>Stress is proportional to strain only within the elastic limit (Hooke's law, σ = Eε).</td></tr><tr><td>The stress–strain curve has six regions: proportional, elastic, yield, plastic, ultimate, fracture.</td></tr><tr><td>Ductile materials deform plastically before breaking; brittle ones break suddenly near the elastic limit.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Stress and pressure are the same.' ✅ Both have units N/m², but stress is the INTERNAL resisting force per unit area produced in a body, while pressure is an EXTERNAL force per unit area applied by a fluid.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Strain has units.' ✅ Strain is dimensionless — it is a ratio of lengths (or volumes/angles). This is essential for dimensional analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Elasticity means materials are always elastic.' ✅ Every material is elastic only up to its elastic limit; beyond it, plastic deformation occurs.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Stress equals force.' ✅ Stress is force PER unit area (σ = F/A). Doubling the area halves the stress for the same force.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The stress–strain graph is a straight line everywhere.' ✅ Only up to the proportional limit. After that it curves at the yield point and beyond.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A ductile material breaks near the elastic limit.' ✅ DUCTILE materials deform plastically and stretch before fracturing; brittle materials break with little warning near the elastic limit.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Stress (σ = F/A) and strain (ε = Δl/l) describe how solids respond to deforming forces. Within the elastic limit, σ = Eε (Hooke's law). Elastic bodies recover their shape; plastic bodies deform permanently. The stress–strain curve shows the proportional limit, elastic limit, yield point, ultimate strength and fracture — ductile materials deform plastically first, brittle materials shatter suddenly.</p><hr><h2>Stress, Strain, Elasticity and Plasticity</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Stress (σ):** the internal resisting force per unit area set up in a body when external forces are applied: σ = F/A. Empirical units are N/m² or Pa (pascal). Normal stress = force perpendicular to the area (tension/compression); shear stress = force parallel to the area.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Strain (ε):** the fractional deformation produced: strain = change in dimension / original dimension. It is dimensionless. Linear strain = Δl/l, shear strain = θ (angle of shear), volumetric strain = ΔV/V.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Elasticity and plasticity:** elasticity is the property of a body to regain its original shape and size when the deforming force is removed. Plasticity is the property of remaining permanently deformed after the deforming force is removed. Bodies are elastic below the elastic limit and plastic beyond it.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Hooke's law in stress-strain form:** within the elastic limit, stress ∝ strain: σ = Eε, where E is Young's modulus (N/m²). This is the generalised Hooke's law — stress and strain are proportional until the elastic limit.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Stress-strain curve regions:** (1) linear (proportional) region up to the proportional limit; (2) elastic region up to the elastic limit (still recovers fully); (3) yielding at the yield point (permanent deformation begins); (4) plastic deformation region where the material flows; (5) ultimate tensile strength (maximum stress), then (6) fracture/breaking point.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — a wire under tension:** a steel wire of length 2 m and cross-section 1 mm² (10⁻⁶ m²) carries a 20 kg load. F = 196 N, σ = 196/10⁻⁶ = 1.96 × 10⁸ Pa. If E_steel = 2 × 10¹¹ Pa, strain ε = σ/E = 9.8 × 10⁻⁴, so extension Δl = 9.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 2 = 1.96 mm.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Thermal Stress:** If a wire is constrained and temperature changes, stress develops: $σ = EαΔT$, where α is the coefficient of linear expansion. This is crucial in bridge construction and railway tracks.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Types of stress & strain (NEB focus):** tensile/compressive (longitudinal), shear (tangential), and volume (hydrostatic). Each has its own modulus: Young's modulus E = σ_t/ε_l, shear modulus G = τ/θ, bulk modulus B = −P/(ΔV/V).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Ductile vs brittle materials:** ductile (copper, mild steel) show a long plastic region — they deform before breaking and are used for wires. Brittle (glass, cast iron) show little or no plastic region — they break suddenly near the elastic limit, which is why glass shatters.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Stress is force per unit area; strain is fractional deformation (dimensionless).</td></tr><tr><td>Elasticity = recovery of shape when the force is removed; plasticity = permanent deformation.</td></tr><tr><td>Stress is proportional to strain only within the elastic limit (Hooke's law, σ = Eε).</td></tr><tr><td>The stress–strain curve has six regions: proportional, elastic, yield, plastic, ultimate, fracture.</td></tr><tr><td>Ductile materials deform plastically before breaking; brittle ones break suddenly near the elastic limit.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Stress and pressure are the same.' ✅ Both have units N/m², but stress is the INTERNAL resisting force per unit area produced in a body, while pressure is an EXTERNAL force per unit area applied by a fluid.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Strain has units.' ✅ Strain is dimensionless — it is a ratio of lengths (or volumes/angles). This is essential for dimensional analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Elasticity means materials are always elastic.' ✅ Every material is elastic only up to its elastic limit; beyond it, plastic deformation occurs.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Stress equals force.' ✅ Stress is force PER unit area (σ = F/A). Doubling the area halves the stress for the same force.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The stress–strain graph is a straight line everywhere.' ✅ Only up to the proportional limit. After that it curves at the yield point and beyond.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A ductile material breaks near the elastic limit.' ✅ DUCTILE materials deform plastically and stretch before fracturing; brittle materials break with little warning near the elastic limit.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Stress (σ = F/A) and strain (ε = Δl/l) describe how solids respond to deforming forces. Within the elastic limit, σ = Eε (Hooke's law). Elastic bodies recover their shape; plastic bodies deform permanently. The stress–strain curve shows the proportional limit, elastic limit, yield point, ultimate strength and fracture — ductile materials deform plastically first, brittle materials shatter suddenly.</p><hr><h2>Elastic Moduli — Young, Bulk, Shear</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Young's modulus (E):** the ratio of longitudinal stress to longitudinal strain: E = (F/A)/(Δl/l) = Fl/(AΔl). It measures resistance to stretching/compression. For steel, E ≈ 2×10¹¹ N/m²; for aluminium ≈ 7×10¹⁰ N/m²; for rubber ≈ 5×10⁶ N/m².</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Bulk modulus (B):** the ratio of volume stress (pressure) to volume strain: B = −P/(ΔV/V). It measures resistance to uniform compression. The minus sign keeps B positive (ΔV is negative under pressure). For water B ≈ 2.2×10⁹ Pa; steel B ≈ 1.6×10¹¹ Pa.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Shear modulus / modulus of rigidity (G or η):** the ratio of shear stress to shear strain: G = τ/θ = (F/A)/θ. It measures resistance to change of shape without change of volume. For steel G ≈ 8×10¹⁰ N/m².</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Key relation between moduli (for isotropic materials):** E = 2G(1 + σ) and E = 3B(1 − 2σ), where σ is Poisson's ratio. Combining them: 9/B = 3/G + 1/E or E = 9BG/(3B + G). These links let you compute one modulus from the others.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — Young's modulus:** a wire of length 2 m and area 1 mm² extends 1 mm under a 50 kg load. F = 490 N, σ = 490/10⁻⁶ = 4.9×10⁸ Pa, ε = 10⁻³/2 = 5×10⁻⁴, E = 4.9×10⁸/5×10⁻⁴ = 9.8×10¹¹ ≈ 10¹¹ N/m².</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — bulk modulus:** 10⁵ Pa pressure on 1 m³ of water changes its volume by 4.5×10⁻⁵ m³. B = 10⁵/(4.5×10⁻⁵) = 2.2×10⁹ Pa.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Compressibility:** the reciprocal of bulk modulus: k = 1/B. Fluids with high B are hard to compress; gases have very low B.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Which modulus to use?** stretching a wire → Young's modulus; squeezing a sphere → bulk modulus; twisting/sliding layers → shear modulus. Choose by the type of deformation.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>E, B, G all have units N/m² (Pa) — they differ by the type of deformation they describe.</td></tr><tr><td>E (stretching), B (volume), G (shape) measure the corresponding resistance of a material.</td></tr><tr><td>The three moduli are linked: E = 2G(1+σ) and E = 3B(1−2σ).</td></tr><tr><td>Liquids and gases have G = 0 (they cannot resist shear) — only solids have a shear modulus.</td></tr><tr><td>Compressibility is the reciprocal of B.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'All three moduli have different units.' ✅ All three are measured in N/m² (Pa) — same units, different deformation types.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Bulk modulus applies only to liquids.' ✅ It applies to solids and gases too — any material under uniform pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Shear strain is a length ratio.' ✅ Shear strain is the ANGLE of displacement θ (radians), not a length ratio.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Young's modulus and stiffness k are the same.' ✅ Y and k connect through geometry: E = Fl/(AΔl) while k = F/Δl, so E = kL/A.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Liquids have Young's modulus.' ✅ Liquids flow; they have NO shear modulus and no Young's modulus for stretching — only bulk modulus.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'B increases when you compress a solid.' ✅ B is a constant MATERIAL property within the elastic range; it does not change with the applied pressure.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The three elastic moduli — Young's (E), bulk (B), and shear (G) — quantify how materials resist stretching, uniform compression, and shape change respectively. Each is stress/strain with units N/m². They are related by E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ). Solids possess all three; fluids have only bulk modulus.</p><hr><h2>Elastic modulus: Young modulus, bulk modulus, shear modulus</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Stress and strain.** Stress is the restoring force per unit area (F/A) and strain is the fractional deformation (ΔL/L); within the elastic limit, stress is proportional to strain (Hooke's law).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Young's modulus.** Y = longitudinal stress ÷ longitudinal strain = FL/AΔL; it measures a material's resistance to stretching or compression along its length.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Bulk modulus.** K = −P ÷ (ΔV/V); the negative sign shows volume decreases under pressure, and its reciprocal is the compressibility of the material.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Shear modulus.** G = shear stress ÷ shear strain = (F/A) ÷ (Δx/L); it measures resistance to change of shape at constant volume.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Poisson's ratio.** σ = −(lateral strain ÷ longitudinal strain); for most stable materials it lies between 0 and 0.5, and elastic potential energy per unit volume is ½ stress × strain.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Elastic modulus</td></tr><tr><td>• Young modulus</td></tr><tr><td>• Bulk modulus</td></tr><tr><td>• Shear modulus</td></tr><tr><td>E, B, G all have units N/m² (Pa) — they differ by the type of deformation they describe.</td></tr><tr><td>E (stretching), B (volume), G (shape) measure the corresponding resistance of a material.</td></tr><tr><td>The three moduli are linked: E = 2G(1+σ) and E = 3B(1−2σ).</td></tr><tr><td>Liquids and gases have G = 0 (they cannot resist shear) — only solids have a shear modulus.</td></tr><tr><td>Compressibility is the reciprocal of B.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ stress = f/a, strain = δl/l (dimensionless). hooke's law (elastic limit): stress ∝ strain..</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'All three moduli have different units.' ✅ All three are measured in N/m² (Pa) — same units, different deformation types.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Bulk modulus applies only to liquids.' ✅ It applies to solids and gases too — any material under uniform pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Shear strain is a length ratio.' ✅ Shear strain is the ANGLE of displacement θ (radians), not a length ratio.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Young's modulus and stiffness k are the same.' ✅ Y and k connect through geometry: E = Fl/(AΔl) while k = F/Δl, so E = kL/A.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Liquids have Young's modulus.' ✅ Liquids flow; they have NO shear modulus and no Young's modulus for stretching — only bulk modulus.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'B increases when you compress a solid.' ✅ B is a constant MATERIAL property within the elastic range; it does not change with the applied pressure.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The three elastic moduli — Young's (E), bulk (B), and shear (G) — quantify how materials resist stretching, uniform compression, and shape change respectively. Each is stress/strain with units N/m². They are related by E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ). Solids possess all three; fluids have only bulk modulus.</p><hr><h2>Elastic Modulus (Young, Bulk, Shear)</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Young's modulus (E):** the ratio of longitudinal stress to longitudinal strain: E = (F/A)/(Δl/l) = Fl/(AΔl). It measures resistance to stretching/compression. For steel, E ≈ 2×10¹¹ N/m²; for aluminium ≈ 7×10¹⁰ N/m²; for rubber ≈ 5×10⁶ N/m².</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Bulk modulus (B):** the ratio of volume stress (pressure) to volume strain: B = −P/(ΔV/V). It measures resistance to uniform compression. The minus sign keeps B positive (ΔV is negative under pressure). For water B ≈ 2.2×10⁹ Pa; steel B ≈ 1.6×10¹¹ Pa.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Shear modulus / modulus of rigidity (G or η):** the ratio of shear stress to shear strain: G = τ/θ = (F/A)/θ. It measures resistance to change of shape without change of volume. For steel G ≈ 8×10¹⁰ N/m².</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Key relation between moduli (for isotropic materials):** E = 2G(1 + σ) and E = 3B(1 − 2σ), where σ is Poisson's ratio. Combining them: 9/B = 3/G + 1/E or E = 9BG/(3B + G). These links let you compute one modulus from the others.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — Young's modulus:** a wire of length 2 m and area 1 mm² extends 1 mm under a 50 kg load. F = 490 N, σ = 490/10⁻⁶ = 4.9×10⁸ Pa, ε = 10⁻³/2 = 5×10⁻⁴, E = 4.9×10⁸/5×10⁻⁴ = 9.8×10¹¹ ≈ 10¹¹ N/m².</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — bulk modulus:** 10⁵ Pa pressure on 1 m³ of water changes its volume by 4.5×10⁻⁵ m³. B = 10⁵/(4.5×10⁻⁵) = 2.2×10⁹ Pa.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Compressibility:** the reciprocal of bulk modulus: k = 1/B. Fluids with high B are hard to compress; gases have very low B.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Which modulus to use?** stretching a wire → Young's modulus; squeezing a sphere → bulk modulus; twisting/sliding layers → shear modulus. Choose by the type of deformation.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>E, B, G all have units N/m² (Pa) — they differ by the type of deformation they describe.</td></tr><tr><td>E (stretching), B (volume), G (shape) measure the corresponding resistance of a material.</td></tr><tr><td>The three moduli are linked: E = 2G(1+σ) and E = 3B(1−2σ).</td></tr><tr><td>Liquids and gases have G = 0 (they cannot resist shear) — only solids have a shear modulus.</td></tr><tr><td>Compressibility is the reciprocal of B.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'All three moduli have different units.' ✅ All three are measured in N/m² (Pa) — same units, different deformation types.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Bulk modulus applies only to liquids.' ✅ It applies to solids and gases too — any material under uniform pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Shear strain is a length ratio.' ✅ Shear strain is the ANGLE of displacement θ (radians), not a length ratio.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Young's modulus and stiffness k are the same.' ✅ Y and k connect through geometry: E = Fl/(AΔl) while k = F/Δl, so E = kL/A.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Liquids have Young's modulus.' ✅ Liquids flow; they have NO shear modulus and no Young's modulus for stretching — only bulk modulus.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'B increases when you compress a solid.' ✅ B is a constant MATERIAL property within the elastic range; it does not change with the applied pressure.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The three elastic moduli — Young's (E), bulk (B), and shear (G) — quantify how materials resist stretching, uniform compression, and shape change respectively. Each is stress/strain with units N/m². They are related by E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ). Solids possess all three; fluids have only bulk modulus.</p><hr><h2>Poisson's Ratio</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Poisson's ratio (σ):** when a body is stretched (or compressed), it becomes thinner (or thicker) in the perpendicular directions. Poisson's ratio is the ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain: σ = −(lateral strain)/(longitudinal strain) = −(β/α). The minus sign keeps σ positive because lateral strain is opposite in sign to longitudinal strain.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Why it is dimensionless:** Poisson's ratio divides one strain by another — strain is dimensionless, so σ has no units and no dimensions. Its value for most materials lies between 0 and 0.5.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Typical values:** cork ≈ 0 (no lateral change), concrete ≈ 0.1–0.2, steel ≈ 0.25–0.3, copper ≈ 0.33, aluminium ≈ 0.34, rubber ≈ 0.5 (nearly incompressible). A hypothetical material with σ = 0.5 would conserve volume exactly.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Worked example — wire stretch:** a wire of length 2 m and diameter 1 mm is stretched by 2 mm. Longitudinal strain = 2×10⁻³/2 = 10⁻³. If σ = 0.3, lateral strain = −0.3×10⁻³, so diameter change = 0.3×10⁻³ × 1 mm = 3×10⁻⁴ mm = 0.3 μm (a fractional diameter change of 3×10⁻⁴).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Relation with the moduli:** σ links Young's modulus E, bulk modulus B and shear modulus G: E = 2G(1 + σ) and E = 3B(1 − 2σ). From these: 1/σ can be used to check material consistency, and σ = (3B − E)/(6B).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Connection to volume change:** the fractional volume change under uniaxial stretch is ΔV/V = ε(1 − 2σ). For a perfectly incompressible material (σ = 0.5), ΔV/V = 0 — the volume is unchanged.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Limits on σ:** the thermodynamic bound is −1 < σ < 0.5. Nearly all common materials have 0 < σ < 0.5. Materials with negative Poisson's ratio (auxetics) actually thicken when stretched — rare and exotic.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Applications:** σ matters in designing cables, stretching films, and geological materials; cork (σ≈0) is chosen for bottle stoppers because it doesn't change width when squeezed, so it seals without expanding.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Poisson's ratio is the (negative) ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain — always dimensionless.</td></tr><tr><td>σ typically lies between 0 and 0.5 for isotropic materials.</td></tr><tr><td>σ links E, B and G: E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ).</td></tr><tr><td>σ = 0.5 means incompressible (volume is conserved); σ = 0 means no lateral change (cork).</td></tr><tr><td>ΔV/V = ε(1 − 2σ) — volume change during stretching depends only on σ and strain.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Poisson's ratio has units of strain.' ✅ It is a RATIO of two strains — dimensionless.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Poisson's ratio can be greater than 1.' ✅ No — the thermodynamic limit is 0.5 for isotropic materials (a few exotic anisotropic materials exceed 0.5 but never 1).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Lateral strain equals longitudinal strain.' ✅ Lateral strain = −σ × longitudinal strain, with σ typically between 0 and 0.5.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'When stretched, volume always increases.' ✅ If σ = 0.5 the volume is constant; for σ < 0.5 the volume increases slightly (ΔV/V = ε(1−2σ)).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Cork and rubber have similar σ.' ✅ Cork ≈ 0, rubber ≈ 0.5 — at opposite ends of the range.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Poisson's ratio is a force.' ✅ It is a pure, dimensionless ratio of deformation (strain) — not a force or modulus.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Poisson's ratio σ = −(lateral strain)/(longitudinal strain) is a dimensionless material constant between 0 and 0.5 describing how a body thins when stretched. It links the elastic moduli through E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ). σ = 0.5 corresponds to an incompressible material, while cork (σ ≈ 0) barely changes width.</p><hr><h2>Poisson's Ratio</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Poisson's ratio (σ):** when a body is stretched (or compressed), it becomes thinner (or thicker) in the perpendicular directions. Poisson's ratio is the ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain: σ = −(lateral strain)/(longitudinal strain) = −(β/α). The minus sign keeps σ positive because lateral strain is opposite in sign to longitudinal strain.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Why it is dimensionless:** Poisson's ratio divides one strain by another — strain is dimensionless, so σ has no units and no dimensions. Its value for most materials lies between 0 and 0.5.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Typical values:** cork ≈ 0 (no lateral change), concrete ≈ 0.1–0.2, steel ≈ 0.25–0.3, copper ≈ 0.33, aluminium ≈ 0.34, rubber ≈ 0.5 (nearly incompressible). A hypothetical material with σ = 0.5 would conserve volume exactly.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Worked example — wire stretch:** a wire of length 2 m and diameter 1 mm is stretched by 2 mm. Longitudinal strain = 2×10⁻³/2 = 10⁻³. If σ = 0.3, lateral strain = −0.3×10⁻³, so diameter change = 0.3×10⁻³ × 1 mm = 3×10⁻⁴ mm = 0.3 μm (a fractional diameter change of 3×10⁻⁴).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Relation with the moduli:** σ links Young's modulus E, bulk modulus B and shear modulus G: E = 2G(1 + σ) and E = 3B(1 − 2σ). From these: 1/σ can be used to check material consistency, and σ = (3B − E)/(6B).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Connection to volume change:** the fractional volume change under uniaxial stretch is ΔV/V = ε(1 − 2σ). For a perfectly incompressible material (σ = 0.5), ΔV/V = 0 — the volume is unchanged.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Limits on σ:** the thermodynamic bound is −1 < σ < 0.5. Nearly all common materials have 0 < σ < 0.5. Materials with negative Poisson's ratio (auxetics) actually thicken when stretched — rare and exotic.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Applications:** σ matters in designing cables, stretching films, and geological materials; cork (σ≈0) is chosen for bottle stoppers because it doesn't change width when squeezed, so it seals without expanding.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Poisson's ratio is the (negative) ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain — always dimensionless.</td></tr><tr><td>σ typically lies between 0 and 0.5 for isotropic materials.</td></tr><tr><td>σ links E, B and G: E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ).</td></tr><tr><td>σ = 0.5 means incompressible (volume is conserved); σ = 0 means no lateral change (cork).</td></tr><tr><td>ΔV/V = ε(1 − 2σ) — volume change during stretching depends only on σ and strain.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Poisson's ratio has units of strain.' ✅ It is a RATIO of two strains — dimensionless.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Poisson's ratio can be greater than 1.' ✅ No — the thermodynamic limit is 0.5 for isotropic materials (a few exotic anisotropic materials exceed 0.5 but never 1).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Lateral strain equals longitudinal strain.' ✅ Lateral strain = −σ × longitudinal strain, with σ typically between 0 and 0.5.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'When stretched, volume always increases.' ✅ If σ = 0.5 the volume is constant; for σ < 0.5 the volume increases slightly (ΔV/V = ε(1−2σ)).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Cork and rubber have similar σ.' ✅ Cork ≈ 0, rubber ≈ 0.5 — at opposite ends of the range.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Poisson's ratio is a force.' ✅ It is a pure, dimensionless ratio of deformation (strain) — not a force or modulus.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Poisson's ratio σ = −(lateral strain)/(longitudinal strain) is a dimensionless material constant between 0 and 0.5 describing how a body thins when stretched. It links the elastic moduli through E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ). σ = 0.5 corresponds to an incompressible material, while cork (σ ≈ 0) barely changes width.</p><hr><h2>Elastic Potential Energy</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Elastic Potential Energy (U):** The energy stored in a deformed elastic body (like a spring or stretched wire) due to the work done against internal restoring forces. For a spring, $U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2$.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Energy Density (u):** The elastic potential energy stored per unit volume. For a wire under tension, $u = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{stress} \times \text{strain} = \frac{1}{2} \sigma \epsilon = \frac{1}{2} \frac{\sigma^2}{E} = \frac{1}{2} E \epsilon^2$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Work Done:** The work done to stretch a spring by $x$ is the area under the $F-x$ graph: $W = \int_0^x F dx = \int_0^x kx dx = \frac{1}{2}kx^2$.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Hysteresis:** In some materials, the loading and unloading stress-strain curves do not coincide. The area enclosed by the loop represents energy dissipated as heat per unit volume per cycle. This is crucial for shock absorbers and rubber components.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** Derive $U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2$; calculate energy stored in springs and wires; understand energy density; explain hysteresis loops.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Elastic potential energy is the work done against restoring forces.</td></tr><tr><td>For a spring, $U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2$.</td></tr><tr><td>Energy density $u = \frac{1}{2} \sigma \epsilon$.</td></tr><tr><td>Hysteresis loop area = energy dissipated per unit volume per cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>Toughness = area under the stress-strain curve up to fracture.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Energy stored is $kx^2$.' ✅ It is $\frac{1}{2}kx^2$ — the factor of 1/2 comes from the average force $\bar{F} = (0+kx)/2 = kx/2$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Energy density is $\sigma \epsilon$.' ✅ It is $\frac{1}{2} \sigma \epsilon$ — again, the 1/2 factor from the triangular area under the stress-strain curve.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Hysteresis loop area is energy stored.' ✅ It is energy DISSIPATED (lost as heat) per cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Elastic potential energy is only for springs.' ✅ It applies to any elastic deformation (wires, beams, bulk compression) where work is done against restoring forces.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Elastic potential energy is stored in deformed bodies. For a spring, $U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2$. For a material under stress, energy density $u = \frac{1}{2} \sigma \epsilon$. Hysteresis loops represent energy dissipation, while the area under the stress-strain curve represents toughness.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Hooke's Law and Force Constant</td><td>Hooke's law (F = kx) states that deformation is proportional to applied force within the elastic limit. The spring constant k (N/m) measures stiffness. Springs combine in series (reciprocal sum, softer) and parallel (direct sum, stiffer). The stored elastic energy is ½kx², and the law underpins spring balances, suspension systems, and simple harmonic motion.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Hooke's Law and Force Constant</td><td>Hooke's law (F = kx) states that deformation is proportional to applied force within the elastic limit. The spring constant k (N/m) measures stiffness. Springs combine in series (reciprocal sum, softer) and parallel (direct sum, stiffer). The stored elastic energy is ½kx², and the law underpins spring balances, suspension systems, and simple harmonic motion.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Stress, Strain, Elasticity and Plasticity</td><td>Stress (σ = F/A) and strain (ε = Δl/l) describe how solids respond to deforming forces. Within the elastic limit, σ = Eε (Hooke's law). Elastic bodies recover their shape; plastic bodies deform permanently. The stress–strain curve shows the proportional limit, elastic limit, yield point, ultimate strength and fracture — ductile materials deform plastically first, brittle materials shatter suddenly.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Stress, Strain, Elasticity and Plasticity</td><td>Stress (σ = F/A) and strain (ε = Δl/l) describe how solids respond to deforming forces. Within the elastic limit, σ = Eε (Hooke's law). Elastic bodies recover their shape; plastic bodies deform permanently. The stress–strain curve shows the proportional limit, elastic limit, yield point, ultimate strength and fracture — ductile materials deform plastically first, brittle materials shatter suddenly.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Elastic Moduli — Young, Bulk, Shear</td><td>The three elastic moduli — Young's (E), bulk (B), and shear (G) — quantify how materials resist stretching, uniform compression, and shape change respectively. Each is stress/strain with units N/m². They are related by E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ). Solids possess all three; fluids have only bulk modulus.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Elastic modulus: Young modulus, bulk modulus, shear modulus</td><td>The three elastic moduli — Young's (E), bulk (B), and shear (G) — quantify how materials resist stretching, uniform compression, and shape change respectively. Each is stress/strain with units N/m². They are related by E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ). Solids possess all three; fluids have only bulk modulus.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Elastic Modulus (Young, Bulk, Shear)</td><td>The three elastic moduli — Young's (E), bulk (B), and shear (G) — quantify how materials resist stretching, uniform compression, and shape change respectively. Each is stress/strain with units N/m². They are related by E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ). Solids possess all three; fluids have only bulk modulus.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Poisson's Ratio</td><td>Poisson's ratio σ = −(lateral strain)/(longitudinal strain) is a dimensionless material constant between 0 and 0.5 describing how a body thins when stretched. It links the elastic moduli through E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ). σ = 0.5 corresponds to an incompressible material, while cork (σ ≈ 0) barely changes width.</td></tr><tr><td>9: Poisson's Ratio</td><td>Poisson's ratio σ = −(lateral strain)/(longitudinal strain) is a dimensionless material constant between 0 and 0.5 describing how a body thins when stretched. It links the elastic moduli through E = 2G(1+σ) = 3B(1−2σ). σ = 0.5 corresponds to an incompressible material, while cork (σ ≈ 0) barely changes width.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Elastic Potential Energy</td><td>Elastic potential energy is stored in deformed bodies. For a spring, $U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2$. For a material under stress, energy density $u = \frac{1}{2} \sigma \epsilon$. Hysteresis loops represent energy dissipation, while the area under the stress-strain curve represents toughness.</td></tr></table></body></html>